"""Scene definitions for the Passkeys with Spring Security teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Passkeys with Spring Security',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains passkeys with Spring Security, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] A passkey is a key pair made by the customer's phone for one site's name. [[slnc 300]] The bank keeps only the public key, and the phone signs a fresh challenge at every sign in. [[slnc 300]] Spring Security does the challenges and the checks in a few lines. [[slnc 300]] Requiring a fingerprint, spotting a copied key, and switching off the password are still the bank's decisions. [[slnc 600]] Think of a signet ring that only fits your own bank's wax seal. [[slnc 600]] Our example is an online bank."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online bank lets customers sign in', 'with a password or a passkey.', '', 'The demo runs the real Spring Boot bank', 'and a software phone that builds', 'the same bytes a real one does.', '', 'Ana registers and signs in;', 'a look-alike site tries; Ben loses his phone.'],
         narration="Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online bank lets customers sign in with a password, or with a passkey. [[slnc 500]] The demo runs the real Spring Boot bank, on your own machine, and a phone written in software that builds the same bytes a real phone does. [[slnc 500]] Challenges, keys and signatures are random on every run, so the output shows them as placeholders. [[slnc 500]] Ana registers her phone and signs in. [[slnc 300]] A look-alike site tries to use her passkey. [[slnc 300]] Spring Security's defaults are compared with a hardened bank. [[slnc 300]] And Ben loses his phone."),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Relying party: the site that checks', 'Authenticator: the phone or security key', 'Challenge: a fresh random value, used once', "Origin: the page's real address", 'User verification: a PIN or a fingerprint', 'Counter: how many times the key signed'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] The relying party is the site that checks the passkey: here, the bank. [[slnc 500]] The authenticator is the phone or security key that holds it. [[slnc 500]] A challenge is a fresh random value, used once. [[slnc 500]] The origin is the real address of the page that asked. [[slnc 500]] User verification means a P I N or a fingerprint, not only a touch. [[slnc 500]] And the counter says how many times the key has signed.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['WebAuthn: Web Authentication: the standard for passkeys in browsers', "rp id: relying party id: the site's name a passkey is made for", 'SHA-256: Secure Hash Algorithm, 256 bits: a 32-byte fingerprint of data', "P-256: the elliptic curve a passkey's key pair lives on", 'ES256: ECDSA signatures on P-256 with SHA-256 (COSE algorithm -7)', 'ECDSA: Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm'],
         narration="A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] Web authn is web authentication: the standard that lets a browser use passkeys. WebAuthn4J is the Java library Spring Security builds on. [[slnc 400]] R P id is the relying party id: the name of the site a passkey is made for, here bank dot example. [[slnc 400]] S H A two fifty six is the secure hash algorithm with two hundred and fifty six bits: a thirty two byte fingerprint of any data. [[slnc 400]] P two five six is the elliptic curve that the passkey's key pair lives on. [[slnc 400]] E S two five six means E C D S A signatures on that curve, with S H A two fifty six. Its number is minus seven. [[slnc 400]] E C D S A is the elliptic curve digital signature algorithm."),
    dict(key='05-letters-2', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['EdDSA: Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm (COSE algorithm -8)', 'RS256: RSA signatures with SHA-256 (COSE algorithm -257)', 'RSA: Rivest, Shamir and Adleman: an older public-key system', 'COSE, CBOR: CBOR Object Signing and Encryption; Concise Binary Object Representation', 'EC2, kty, alg, crv: COSE key fields: key type (EC2 = elliptic curve), algorithm, curve', 'JSON: JavaScript Object Notation: the text format of the client data'],
         narration='Number minus eight is the Edwards curve digital signature algorithm. [[slnc 400]] R S two five six, number minus two five seven, means R S A signatures with S H A two fifty six. [[slnc 400]] R S A is named after Rivest, Shamir and Adleman: an older public key system. [[slnc 400]] C B O R, the concise binary object representation, is a compact binary form of data. COSE, C B O R object signing and encryption, uses it to write keys. [[slnc 400]] In a COSE key, k t y is the key type, where E C two means an elliptic curve key. Alg is the algorithm, and c r v is the curve. [[slnc 400]] Jason is JavaScript object notation: the text format of the options and the client data.'),
    dict(key='06-letters-3', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For (continued)',
         body=['PIN: Personal Identification Number', 'HTTP, POST, DELETE: Hypertext Transfer Protocol; its methods to send and to remove', '200, 204, 401: HTTP status codes: OK; done, nothing to return; not signed in', 'JDBC: Java Database Connectivity'],
         narration='A pin is a personal identification number. [[slnc 400]] H T T P is the hypertext transfer protocol. Post sends data to the bank, and delete removes something. [[slnc 400]] Two hundred means O K. Two oh four means done, with nothing to return. Four oh one means not signed in: refused. [[slnc 400]] J D B C is Java database connectivity: how Java talks to a database.'),
    dict(key='07-library-and-bank', kind='figure', title='What the library checks, and what it leaves',
         body='library_and_bank',
         narration="Let's see the whole idea on one picture. [[slnc 400]] On the left, what Spring Security checks for the bank, with no extra code. [[slnc 300]] A challenge is used once, so a replayed answer gets four oh one. [[slnc 300]] The origin must be the bank's, so a look-alike page gets four oh one. [[slnc 300]] And the signature must match the stored public key, so another key gets four oh one. [[slnc 700]] On the right, what its defaults leave to the bank. [[slnc 300]] A touch without a fingerprint is accepted, because user verification is only preferred. [[slnc 300]] And a copied key is accepted, because the counter is stored but never compared. [[slnc 600]] The hardened bank adds a small wrapper, and both are refused. [[slnc 300]] The library checks the bytes. [[slnc 300]] The bank still decides how strict to be."),
    dict(key='08-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='Registering: the options sent, and the COSE key kept',
         body='cose_key',
         narration="First demo: registering a passkey. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the options that Spring Security sends to Ana's browser. [[slnc 300]] A challenge and a user handle, each thirty two random bytes, different on every run. [[slnc 300]] The relying party: bank dot example. [[slnc 300]] The signing algorithms it accepts. [[slnc 300]] User verification: preferred. [[slnc 300]] Remember that word. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the public key that comes back, in the COSE format, seventy seven bytes. [[slnc 300]] It is a small map of numbered labels. [[slnc 300]] Key type two means an elliptic curve key. [[slnc 300]] Algorithm minus seven means E S two five six. [[slnc 300]] Curve one is P two five six. [[slnc 300]] And x and y are the point on the curve, thirty two bytes each. [[slnc 700]] That is all the bank stores, with a counter and a label. [[slnc 300]] A lock, not a key."),
    dict(key='09-act-1-1', kind='console', title='Act One — Registering a passkey: the bank keeps only a public key',
         body="ONE. Registering a passkey: the bank keeps only a public key.\n  THE REQUEST (POST /webauthn/register/options, as ana):\n    challenge          <32 random bytes>\n    rp                 id bank.example, name The Bank\n    user handle        <32 random bytes>\n    algorithms         [-8, -7, -257]\n                       (-8 EdDSA, -7 ES256, -257 RS256)\n    user verification  preferred\n    attestation        none\n  THE RESPONSE (POST /webauthn/register, a new P-256 key pair):\n    credential id      16 bytes\n    attestation        fmt none: no statement about the phone's make\n    COSE public key    77 bytes, starts a5010203262001215820",
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The request: a random challenge, the relying party, a random user handle, three algorithms, user verification preferred, and no attestation. [[slnc 400]] The response: a sixteen byte credential id, and a seventy seven byte public key.'),
    dict(key='10-act-1-2', kind='console', title='Act One — Registering a passkey: the bank keeps only a public key',
         body='ONE. (continued)\n  THE COSE KEY, DECODED (CBOR: label = value):\n    1   kty      = 2  (EC2: an elliptic-curve key)\n    3   alg      = -7  (ES256)\n    -1  crv      = 1  (P-256)\n    -2  x        = 32 bytes  (a point on the curve)\n    -3  y        = 32 bytes  (a point on the curve)\n  THE BANK\'S CHECKS (challenge, type, origin, rp id hash):\n    200 accepted (passkey ana\'s phone registered)\n  WHAT THE BANK STORES (a CredentialRecord):\n    credential id 16 bytes, public key 77 bytes, counter 0\n    label "ana\'s phone", and nothing that can sign\n  A COPY OF THE RECORDS (another key, ana\'s credential id):\n    refused (401): the signature does not match',
         narration="The public key, decoded label by label. [[slnc 400]] The bank's checks answer two hundred: registered. [[slnc 400]] It stores the id, the public key, a counter of zero, and a label. [[slnc 400]] An answer signed with another key under Ana's credential id gets four oh one."),
    dict(key='11-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='How the signature is made, byte by byte',
         body='signed_bytes',
         narration="Second demo: signing in. [[slnc 400]] This picture shows what Ana's phone signs. [[slnc 700]] Box one: the client data, written by the browser. [[slnc 300]] The type, the bank's fresh challenge, and the page's real origin. [[slnc 300]] The challenge is random, so here it is a placeholder. [[slnc 300]] Its S H A two fifty six hash is thirty two bytes. [[slnc 700]] Row two: the authenticator data, written by the phone. [[slnc 300]] The hash of the name bank dot example. [[slnc 300]] The flags byte, zero five: present, and verified. [[slnc 300]] And the counter, one. [[slnc 300]] Thirty seven bytes. [[slnc 700]] Step three: thirty seven plus thirty two is sixty nine bytes. [[slnc 300]] Step four: the phone signs them with its private key. [[slnc 600]] Spring Security redoes the hash, and checks the signature with the stored public key. [[slnc 300]] It answers two hundred. [[slnc 300]] Change one flag after signing, and it answers four oh one."),
    dict(key='12-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — Signing in: a fresh challenge, signed once',
         body='TWO. Signing in: a fresh challenge, signed once.\n  THE REQUEST (POST /webauthn/authenticate/options):\n    challenge          <32 random bytes>\n    new on every call  true\n    rp id              bank.example\n    user verification  preferred\n  STEP 1. THE CLIENT DATA (the browser writes it):\n    {"type":"webauthn.get",\n    "challenge":"<challenge>",\n    "origin":"https://bank.example","crossOrigin":false}\n  STEP 2. THE AUTHENTICATOR DATA (37 bytes, the phone writes it):\n    rp id hash  05be55af508c5555...  SHA-256 of bank.example\n    flags       05  user present + user verified\n    counter     00000001  = 1',
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The request: a random challenge, new on every call, the relying party id, and user verification preferred. [[slnc 400]] Step one, the client data. [[slnc 300]] Step two, the authenticator data: the name hash, flags zero five, counter one.'),
    dict(key='13-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — Signing in: a fresh challenge, signed once',
         body="TWO. (continued)\n  STEP 3. THE SIGNED BYTES (authenticator data + SHA-256):\n    37 + 32 = 69 bytes\n  STEP 4. THE SIGNATURE (ES256, with the private key):\n    <about 70 bytes, different on every run>\n  THE RESPONSE (POST /login/webauthn, Spring Security checks it):\n    200 accepted (signed in as ana with a passkey)\n  THE SAME ANSWER, OR A CHANGED ONE:\n    again, from another browser:      refused (401)\n    again, to the browser that asked: refused (401)\n    another browser's challenge:      refused (401)\n    flags 01 -> 05 after signing:     refused (401)",
         narration="Step three: sixty nine signed bytes. [[slnc 300]] Step four: a signature of about seventy bytes. [[slnc 400]] Spring Security answers two hundred: Ana is signed in. [[slnc 400]] The same answer again, from another browser or from the one that asked: four oh one. [[slnc 300]] Another browser's challenge: four oh one. [[slnc 300]] Flags edited after signing: four oh one."),
    dict(key='14-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='The site rule and the signed origin, worked out',
         body='origin_check',
         narration="Third demo: a look-alike site. [[slnc 400]] The page lives at bank dash example dot test. [[slnc 700]] The top panel is the browser's rule. [[slnc 300]] The page asks for a passkey for bank dot example. [[slnc 300]] Is the page's host equal to that name? [[slnc 200]] No. [[slnc 300]] Does it end with dot bank dot example? [[slnc 200]] No. [[slnc 300]] So the browser refuses, and the phone is never asked. [[slnc 700]] The bottom panel imagines a faulty browser that skipped the rule. [[slnc 300]] It still writes the page's real origin into the client data, and the phone signs it. [[slnc 300]] The bank configured one allowed origin: its own. [[slnc 300]] So Spring Security answers four oh one. [[slnc 700]] The page cannot choose the origin. [[slnc 300]] That is why a passkey cannot be phished."),
    dict(key='15-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — A look-alike site gets nothing it can use',
         body="THREE. A look-alike site gets nothing it can use.\n  THE PAGE (a copy of the bank's page):\n    https://bank-example.test\n  REQUEST 1. A PASSKEY FOR ITS OWN NAME bank-example.test:\n    nothing (the authenticator has no passkey for bank-example.test)\n  REQUEST 2. THE BANK'S REAL CHALLENGE, RP ID bank.example:\n    is bank-example.test equal to bank.example?  no\n    does it end with .bank.example?  no\n    nothing (bank.example is not this page's site)",
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The page. [[slnc 300]] Request one, for its own name: nothing. [[slnc 400]] Request two, for the bank's name: not equal, does not end with it, so nothing."),
    dict(key='16-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — A look-alike site gets nothing it can use',
         body='THREE. (continued)\n  REQUEST 3. A BROWSER THAT SKIPPED THAT RULE:\n    signed client data: "origin":"https://bank-example.test"\n    allowed origins:    [https://bank.example]\n    refused (401)\n  THE SAME PAGE ASKS FOR ANA\'S PASSWORD, AND SHE TYPES IT:\n    replayed at the bank: accepted (signed in as ana with a password)\n  a password can be phished; a passkey cannot',
         narration="Request three, through a browser that skipped the rule. [[slnc 300]] The signed origin is the look-alike's address, not an allowed origin: four oh one. [[slnc 400]] Then the page asks for Ana's password, and she types it. [[slnc 300]] Replayed at the bank, it works. [[slnc 300]] A password can be phished. [[slnc 300]] A passkey cannot."),
    dict(key='17-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='The defaults, and a bank that insists',
         body='counters',
         narration="Fourth demo: user verification, and copies. [[slnc 400]] On the left, Spring Security's defaults. [[slnc 300]] User verification is only preferred. [[slnc 300]] So a touch alone, flags zero one, is accepted. [[slnc 300]] A counter that went back from three to one is accepted. [[slnc 300]] And a copied key is accepted. [[slnc 300]] Spring stores the counter, but does not compare it. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the hardened bank. [[slnc 300]] A small wrapper requires verification, so a touch alone is refused, and a fingerprint is accepted. [[slnc 600]] Then the counter. [[slnc 300]] The bank has seen six. [[slnc 300]] The real phone signs with seven: accepted. [[slnc 300]] The copy also signs with seven. [[slnc 300]] Seven is not above seven, so it is refused, and the passkey is suspended until Ana confirms it."),
    dict(key='18-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — User verification, and counters against copies',
         body="FOUR. User verification, and counters against copies.\n  SPRING SECURITY'S DEFAULTS (user verification preferred):\n    touch only, flags 01:\n      accepted (signed in as ana with a passkey)  <- NOT REQUIRED\n    counter 3, then 1 (restored from a backup):\n      accepted (signed in as ana with a passkey)\n    a copied key, counter 2, as the real phone would:\n      accepted (signed in as ana with a passkey)  <- NOT NOTICED\n  HARDENED (HardenedRelyingParty around Spring Security's):\n    user verification  required\n    touch only, flags 01:\n      refused (user verification required)\n    touch and fingerprint, flags 05:\n      accepted (signed in as ana with a passkey)",
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] The defaults: a touch only, accepted. [[slnc 300]] A counter going back, accepted. [[slnc 300]] A copied key, accepted, and not noticed. [[slnc 400]] The hardened bank: user verification required. [[slnc 300]] A touch only, refused. [[slnc 300]] With a fingerprint, accepted.'),
    dict(key='19-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — User verification, and counters against copies',
         body='FOUR. (continued)\n  THE COUNTERS (the bank keeps the last one it saw):\n    the bank has seen 6\n    real phone:  counter 7, 7 > 6?  yes\n      accepted (signed in as ana with a passkey)\n    copied key:  counter 7, 7 > 7?  no\n      refused (counter 7 is not above 7: a cloned key?)\n    real phone again, counter 8:\n      refused (passkey suspended after a counter went backwards)\n  A SYNCED PASSKEY (flags 1d: + backup eligible + backed up):\n    flags 1d, counter 0: accepted (signed in as ben with a passkey)\n    flags 1d, counter 0: accepted (signed in as ben with a passkey)\n  a synced passkey always reports 0: a copy cannot be spotted',
         narration='The counters. [[slnc 300]] Seen six. [[slnc 300]] The real phone with seven, accepted. [[slnc 300]] The copy with seven, refused. [[slnc 300]] The real phone with eight, refused: the passkey is suspended. [[slnc 400]] A synced passkey, flags one d, counter zero, accepted twice. [[slnc 300]] A copy of it cannot be spotted.'),
    dict(key='20-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='Every way in, and the password beside the passkeys',
         body='ways_in',
         narration="Fifth demo: the bill. [[slnc 400]] On the left, Ben loses his phone. [[slnc 300]] His new laptop has no passkey, so nothing. [[slnc 300]] His security key signs him in. [[slnc 300]] With it, he deletes the lost phone's passkey, and the bank answers two oh four. [[slnc 300]] A thief with the phone and its fingerprint now gets four oh one. [[slnc 700]] On the right, the password. [[slnc 300]] Here a green answer is the good news only when it is a refusal. [[slnc 300]] Ana's password, still on, is accepted, and a look-alike page can collect it. [[slnc 300]] Once she has two passkeys, the bank switches it off. [[slnc 300]] Ben, with no passkey, keeps his, for now. [[slnc 700]] The account is only as strong as its weakest way in."),
    dict(key='21-act-5-1', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill: recovery, lost devices and the move away from passwords',
         body="FIVE. The bill: recovery, lost devices and the move away from passwords.\n  BEN'S WAYS IN:\n    2 passkeys: his phone and a security key, each with a label\n  BEN'S PHONE IS LOST:\n    new laptop:\n      nothing (the authenticator has no passkey for bank.example)\n    security key:\n      accepted (signed in as ben with a passkey)\n    DELETE /webauthn/register/<the phone's credential id>:\n      204 (the lost phone's passkey is removed)\n    a thief with the phone and its fingerprint:\n      refused (401)",
         narration="Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] Ben's two passkeys. [[slnc 300]] His phone is lost: the laptop gets nothing, the security key is accepted, deleting the phone's passkey answers two oh four, and the thief with the phone gets four oh one."),
    dict(key='22-act-5-2', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill: recovery, lost devices and the move away from passwords',
         body="FIVE. (continued)\n  recovery is now the weakest way in: guard it like a passkey\n  ANA'S OLD PASSWORD, STILL SWITCHED ON:\n    accepted (signed in as ana with a password)  <- THE WEAKEST WAY IN\n  ANA'S PASSWORD, SWITCHED OFF (she has 2 passkeys):\n    refused (401)\n  BEN, WITH NO PASSKEY, KEEPS HIS PASSWORD:\n    accepted (signed in as ben with a password)\n  Spring Security's WebAuthn support, built on WebAuthn4J",
         narration="Recovery is now the weakest way in. [[slnc 400]] Ana's password, still on: accepted. [[slnc 300]] Switched off once she has two passkeys: four oh one. [[slnc 300]] Ben, with no passkey, keeps his password. [[slnc 400]] All of the checks came from Spring Security's web authn support."),
    dict(key='23-costs', kind='bullets', title='What It Costs',
         body=['Recovery becomes the weak point', 'Synced passkeys are as safe as the cloud account', 'Requiring a fingerprint excludes some keys', 'Two passkeys before the password can go', "Spring's WebAuthn support is still young"],
         narration="Nothing is free. [[slnc 400]] Here is what it costs. [[slnc 500]] Recovery becomes the weak point, because an attacker will go after the way back in. [[slnc 500]] A synced passkey is only as safe as the cloud account it syncs through. [[slnc 500]] Requiring a fingerprint or a P I N excludes security keys that cannot ask for one. [[slnc 500]] Customers need two passkeys before the password can go. [[slnc 300]] And Spring Security's passkey support is still young."),
    dict(key='24-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['"Sign in with a passkey" on a bank\'s page', 'A fingerprint prompt naming the site', 'userVerification: preferred in the options', 'An rpId such as bank.example'],
         narration="You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] A sign in with a passkey button, on a bank's page, is this flow. [[slnc 500]] A fingerprint prompt from your phone that names the site is the phone checking the name for you. [[slnc 500]] User verification preferred, in the options a site sends, means a touch without a fingerprint is accepted. [[slnc 500]] And a relying party id like bank dot example is the name every passkey is bound to."),
    dict(key='25-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['A maintained WebAuthn library', 'Require user verification', 'Compare the counter; suspend on a step back', 'Synced passkeys: trust the cloud account', 'Two passkeys before passwords go', 'Design recovery as carefully as sign-in'],
         narration="So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Use a maintained web authn library, like Spring Security's. [[slnc 300]] Require user verification, not just a touch. [[slnc 500]] Compare the signature counter, and suspend a passkey whose counter steps back. [[slnc 300]] Synced passkeys usually report zero, so for them, the cloud account is what you trust. [[slnc 500]] Ask for two passkeys before switching the password off. [[slnc 300]] And design recovery as carefully as sign in."),
    dict(key='26-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=["PasskeySecurity: Spring Security's WebAuthn support, switched on for the bank", 'HardenedRelyingParty: requires verification, compares the counter', 'PasswordSwitchOffFilter: the password ends once a customer has two passkeys', 'SoftwareAuthenticator: the phone in software: the same bytes a real one builds'],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] The bank is a real Spring Boot application; the phone and the browser are small Java classes that build the same bytes. [[slnc 300]] Here are the classes that matter.'),
    dict(key='27-class-1', kind='code', title='PasskeySecurity — one line for the endpoints',
         body='// PasskeySecurity: Spring Security\'s WebAuthn support, switched on for the bank\n.webAuthn(w -> w.rpName("The Bank").rpId(RP_ID).allowedOrigins(ORIGIN));',
         narration='One line switches on the registration and sign in endpoints. [[slnc 300]] The bank states its relying party id, its name, and the one origin its pages come from.'),
    dict(key='28-class-2', kind='code', title='PasskeySecurity — the hardened mode',
         body="// PasskeySecurity: Spring Security's WebAuthn support, switched on for the bank\nspring.setCustomizeRequestOptions(\n        options -> options.userVerification(UserVerificationRequirement.REQUIRED));\nreturn new HardenedRelyingParty(spring, credentials);",
         narration="In the hardened mode, the options ask for user verification as required, not preferred. [[slnc 300]] And Spring's relying party is wrapped."),
    dict(key='29-class-3', kind='code', title='HardenedRelyingParty — insist, then compare',
         body='// HardenedRelyingParty: requires verification, compares the counter\nif ((authenticatorData[FLAGS] & USER_VERIFIED) == 0) {\n...\n// Spring Security checks the signature, the challenge, the origin and the relying party id\nPublicKeyCredentialUserEntity user = delegate.authenticate(request);\n...\nif ((storedCounter > 0 || presented > 0) && presented <= storedCounter) {\n    suspended.add(id);',
         narration='It refuses an answer without the verified flag. [[slnc 300]] Then Spring Security checks the signature, the challenge and the origin. [[slnc 300]] Only after that, a counter that did not go up suspends the passkey.'),
    dict(key='30-class-4', kind='code', title='PasswordSwitchOffFilter — two passkeys, no password',
         body='// PasswordSwitchOffFilter: the password ends once a customer has two passkeys\nif (user != null && credentials.findByUserId(user.getId()).size() >= PASSKEYS_NEEDED) {\n    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED,\n            "password sign-in is switched off for this customer");',
         narration='A filter in front of the password form. [[slnc 300]] A customer with two passkeys has a way back in, so the password, which can be phished, is refused.'),
    dict(key='31-class-5', kind='code', title='SoftwareAuthenticator — flags, COSE key, signature',
         body='// SoftwareAuthenticator: the phone in software: the same bytes a real one builds\nint flags = 0x01 | (userVerified ? 0x04 : 0)\n        | (backupEligible ? 0x08 : 0) | (backupState ? 0x10 : 0)\n        | (withCredential ? 0x40 : 0);\n...\nout.writeBytes(Cbor.map(1, 2, 3, -7, -1, 1, -2, x, -3, y));\n...\nsignature.update(signedBytes(authData, clientData));',
         narration='The flags byte adds up present, verified, and the two backup bits of a synced passkey. [[slnc 300]] The public key is the five label COSE map. [[slnc 300]] And the signature covers the sixty nine signed bytes.'),
    dict(key='32-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Store the passkeys with', "Spring Security's JDBC repositories."],
         narration="That's passkeys with Spring Security. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] A passkey proves that a customer holds a private key made for the bank's name, so it cannot be phished, and the bank still decides about user verification, copies, recovery and the password. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Store the passkeys in a database, with the J D B C repositories that Spring Security provides. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

